    },
    {
      "scene_id": "S05_TheWheel",
      "frame_range": [
        1020,
        1590
      ],
      "transcript_segment": "So can we do better? Can we get the scheduling down to a constant time complexity? Yes, by using hierarchical timing wheel. Picture a simple clock face with 60 slots. If the task is due in 5 seconds, you don't sort it. You just drop it directly to that 5 second bucket.",
      "narrative_goal": "Introduce the O(1) Timing Wheel",
      "viewer_takeaway": "Direct indexing is faster than sorting",
      "visual_story": {
        "setup": {
          "description": "Large circular clock-like gear with 60 numbered slots",
          "mood": "satisfyingly simple"
        },
        "build_sequence": [
          {
            "at_frame": 1150,
            "action": "Timing Wheel appears with a bouncy entrance",
            "element": "timing_wheel",
            "technique": "scale-spring",
            "effects": [
              "gear-rotation-start"
            ],
            "rationale": "New hero element"
          },
          {
            "at_frame": 1350,
            "action": "A task (orb) flies and drops perfectly into slot #5",
            "element": "task_drop",
            "technique": "parabolic-arc",
            "effects": [
              "slot-highlight",
              "sparkle-on-impact"
            ],
            "rationale": "Show O(1) direct placement"
          }
        ],
        "hero_moment": {
          "what": "Multiple tasks dropping into different slots simultaneously with NO sorting movement",
          "frame_range": [
            1450,
            1550
          ],
          "treatment": "Satisfying 'click' animations"
        },
        "elements_on_stage_at_end": [
          "timing_wheel"
        ]
      },
      "element_positions": {
        "timing_wheel": {
          "x_percent": 50,
          "y_percent": 50
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "S06_Hierarchy",
      "frame_range": [
        1590,
        2220
      ],
      "transcript_segment": "But the real genius is in the hierarchy. What if the task is due in an hour? You don't build a massive wheel. You use a second larger wheel acting like a minute hand. This outer wheel doesn't execute tasks, it just holds them. And when the minute hand finally ticks, it takes the task in that bucket and cascades them down to the second wheel to be executed.",
      "narrative_goal": "Explain the hierarchical cascading mechanism",
      "viewer_takeaway": "Multiple wheels handle different time scales without massive memory",
      "visual_story": {
        "setup": {
          "description": "Two concentric wheels: Outer (Minutes) and Inner (Seconds)",
          "mood": "mechanical wonder"
        },
        "build_sequence": [
          {
            "at_frame": 1650,
            "action": "Outer wheel zooms in behind the inner wheel",
            "element": "outer_wheel",
            "technique": "fade-grow",
            "effects": [
              "secondary-color-theme"
            ],
            "rationale": "Establishing hierarchy"
          },
          {
            "at_frame": 1800,
            "action": "Tasks land in the Outer Wheel",
            "element": "outer_tasks",
            "technique": "drop",
            "effects": [],
            "rationale": "Long-term storage"
          },
          {
            "at_frame": 2050,
            "action": "Outer wheel 'ticks', tasks fall into the Inner wheel",
            "element": "cascade_animation",
            "technique": "gravity-fall",
            "effects": [
              "motion-trails",
              "radial-spread"
            ],
            "rationale": "Visualizing the 'cascade' concept"
          }
        ],
        "hero_moment": {
          "what": "The cascade of tasks flowing from the large outer gear to the small inner gear like liquid",
          "frame_range": [
            2100,
            2200
          ],
          "treatment": "Particle cascade with glow"
        },
        "elements_on_stage_at_end": [
          "inner_wheel",
          "outer_wheel"
        ]
      },
      "element_positions": {
        "inner_wheel": {
          "x_percent": 50,
          "y_percent": 60
        },
        "outer_wheel": {
          "x_percent": 50,
          "y_percent": 35
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "scene_id": "S07_RealWorld",
      "frame_range": [
        2250,
        2640
      ],
      "transcript_segment": "It is a literal mechanical clock mechanism running in your code. No sorting, just rotating. This isn't just a theory also. This is the exact architecture Apache Kafka and Netty used to handle massive throughput without lag.",
